namespace Death {
//###==##====#=====--==~--~=~- --- -- -  -  -   -

/** @page level-packs Level packs
@brief Installing, playing and removing packs of levels made by the community

@tableofcontents

@m_class{m-noindent}

A level pack is an archive of original Jazz Jackrabbit 2 files --- levels, episodes, tile sets, music, scripts and
anything else the scripts load --- as the level archive of the community offers them. An installed pack is converted
the same way as the original game, so its levels are ready to play, and it can be removed again without leaving
anything behind. Level packs are compiled only if `WITH_LEVEL_PACKS` and the game has an HTTP backend (WinHTTP on
Windows, libcurl elsewhere).

@section level-packs-playing Playing

In the main menu choose <b>Play Story</b> → <b>Level Packs</b>. The installed packs are shown as tiles with a
preview, several in a row if the screen is wide enough. A pack shows its details, its episodes and all its levels,
any of which can be started, and it can be removed there too. Episodes of the packs are listed in <b>Play Story</b>
along with the episodes of the game, their progress is saved the same way.

@m_class{m-noindent}

A pack whose levels need a tile set or music that neither the pack nor the game has is marked with `!`, its levels
may look or sound wrong.

@section level-packs-names Names of levels

A level of a pack is named with a `+` and the identifier of the pack, so it never takes the place of a level of the
game or of another pack:

-   @cpp "+1234/battle1" @ce --- a level of the pack @cpp 1234 @ce that belongs to no episode
-   @cpp "+1234_hh18/hh18_level01" @ce --- a level of the episode `hh18` of the same pack

@m_class{m-noindent}

The identifier is the number of the pack in the level database, so a server playlist names the same level on every
machine. A pack installed from a file of its own gets @cpp local- @ce and a part of the hash of the archive instead.
Links between the levels of a pack stay inside the pack.

@section level-packs-cli Installing from the command line

Packs can be managed without starting the game, which is how a dedicated server gets them too:

Argument | Description
-------- | -----------
`--install-pack <file>` | Installs a pack from a ZIP archive, or installs it again. Can be given more times
`--remove-pack <id>` | Removes a pack, the identifier can also be given as a level name, e.g. @cpp "+1234/battle1" @ce
`--list-packs` | Lists the installed packs with their episodes and levels

@m_class{m-noindent}

The game exits once they're done, unless `--server` is given too, then the server starts afterwards. A running
server installs and removes packs with the `/packs` command, see @ref multiplayer-commands. A client that doesn't
have the pack of the level being played gets the level from the server, as any other level.

@code{.sh}
./jazz2 --install-pack Holiday-Hare-18.zip --list-packs
./jazz2 --install-pack Battle-Pack.zip --server server.json
@endcode

@section level-packs-storage Where packs are kept

-   The original files of all packs are kept in `LevelPacks` of the content directory the game can write to (see
    @ref Jazz2::ContentResolver::GetWritableContentPath()), each of them once. A file that more packs have is kept
    only once, and a file that's the same as the one of the original game isn't kept at all.
-   Each installed pack has a record in `LevelPacks/Packs`, the files it has are listed there.
-   The converted levels and tile sets are in `Cache/LevelPacks`. They're converted again from the original files
    when the cache is rebuilt or a new version of the game converts levels differently.
-   Removing a pack removes its record and its converted levels, and every kept file no other pack has.

@m_class{m-noindent}

Only ZIP archives are installed, stored or compressed by Deflate. Only files the game or a script can use are taken
from them (levels, tile sets, episodes, scripts, music, sounds, sprites and images), the folders in the archive don't
matter. An archive with two different files of the same name is refused.

@section level-packs-scripts Scripts

Scripts of the levels are used only if the game is compiled with scripting. A script finds the files of its pack by
their original names, the files it includes, the sounds, sprites and images it loads and the levels it takes layers
of. Files that the pack doesn't have are looked for in the original game as before.

@section level-packs-editor Level editor

The levels of the installed packs are listed in the file dialogs of the level editor under <b>Level packs</b>, with
the names of the packs, their episodes and their levels. They can be opened, but not saved where they are --- they
would be replaced by the next conversion. <b>Save As</b> puts a copy into the user content instead, together with the
tile sets and the music of the pack it uses, so the copy works even once the pack is removed.

@section level-packs-api Level database API

@m_class{m-block m-warning}

@par Proposal
The level database doesn't offer this yet, it's what the game is going to expect. The names and the shape of it are
open to change.

All responses are JSON in UTF-8, served over HTTPS. Images are PNG, archives are ZIP. Every response, images and
archives included, should have `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *`, so the web version of the game can use it too.

@subsection level-packs-api-search Searching packs

@code{.sh}
GET /api/packs?query=<text>&type=<type>&tag=<tag>&sort=<newest|rating|downloads>&page=<1..>&perPage=<1..100>
@endcode

Every parameter is optional. `type` and `tag` can be given more times, a pack has to have all of them.

@code{.json}
{
    "total": 1234,
    "page": 1,
    "perPage": 48,
    "items": [
        {
            "id": "8123",
            "title": "Holiday Hare '18",
            "authors": [ "Author One", "Author Two" ],
            "types": [ "SinglePlayer", "Episode" ],
            "tags": [ "Christmas", "Contest" ],
            "rating": 8.7,
            "downloads": 15320,
            "updated": "2018-12-24T12:00:00Z",
            "version": "3",
            "size": 7865635,
            "hasScript": true,
            "thumbnail": "https://.../8123/thumbnail.png"
        }
    ]
}
@endcode

@subsection level-packs-api-details Details of a pack

@code{.sh}
GET /api/packs/<id>
@endcode

The same fields as in the search, and:

@code{.json}
{
    "description": "Plain text, paragraphs separated by an empty line",
    "screenshots": [ "https://.../8123/screenshot-1.png" ],
    "levels": [
        { "file": "HH18_level01.j2l", "title": "Penguinvasion", "types": [ "SinglePlayer" ] }
    ],
    "dependencies": [ "4567" ],
    "download": {
        "url": "https://.../8123/v3/HolidayHare18.zip",
        "fileName": "HolidayHare18.zip",
        "size": 7865635,
        "sha256": "lowercase hexadecimal SHA-256 of the archive"
    }
}
@endcode

@subsection level-packs-api-rules Requirements

-   `id` is a string of digits that never changes and isn't used again for another pack.
-   `version` changes whenever the archive does. The game offers an update when it differs from the installed one.
-   The archive is always ZIP. A pack uploaded as RAR or 7z should be offered repacked as ZIP. `size` and `sha256`
    are of the archive the URL returns, the game refuses an archive that doesn't match them.
-   `thumbnail` is 256 × 144 pixels, screenshots are at most 1280 pixels wide.
-   `types` take these values: `SinglePlayer`, `Cooperative`, `Battle`, `CaptureTheFlag`, `Race`, `TreasureHunt`,
    `Episode`, `TileSet`, `Mutator`, `Other`.
-   `dependencies` are packs that have to be installed for this one to work, e.g. a pack of tile sets.
-   An error is answered with an HTTP status other than 200 and `{ "error": "Description" }`.
*/

}
